<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Melno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1422">1422</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Melno, Peace of Lake Melno · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gollub-war">Gollub War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Melno was signed on 27 September 1422 near Lake Melno between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gollub-war">Gollub War</a>. The Order gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> permanently, and the treaty fixed the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, which remained essentially unchanged for about five centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno.jpg" alt="Treaty of Melno" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S. Bollmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teutonic_Order_1410.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-peace-of-thorn">First Peace of Thorn</a> of 1411 had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> only for the lifetimes of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Grand Duke Vytautas, both elderly men. Vytautas then claimed the whole northern bank of the Neman River, including the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> (Klaipėda). When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, ruled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, Jogaila and Vytautas invaded the Teutonic state in July 1422. Grand Master Paul von Rusdorf could mount no adequate defence, but Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> sought to end the war before reinforcements arrived from the Empire. A truce followed on 17 September, and eight envoys from each side met at the Polish camp near the lake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>For the first time the Knights renounced all territorial, political and missionary claims against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The border ran from the wilderness of Suvalkija to Nemirseta on the Baltic, leaving the Knights the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>. Lithuania kept about 15 kilometres of coast between Palanga and Šventoji, though it never developed a real harbour there; for the Knights the strip cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> off from their branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. Poland received Nieszawa and half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> channel from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drweca">Drwęca</a> and gave up claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Culmerland</a> and the Michelauer Land, results described as a disappointment for Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Rusdorf tried to renegotiate, but at Käsmark Sigismund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> agreed on 30 March 1423 that the emperor would drop his support for the Knights and Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> would stop aiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>. The treaty was ratified at Veliuona in May 1423, with some 120 seals, and approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> on 10 July. It ended warfare between the Knights and Lithuania that had gone on since the 13th century, though Polish-Teutonic disputes continued. The border was redrawn more precisely in 1532 and 1545 and survived until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; its southern part still divides Lithuania from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Melno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ransoms, war taxes and further wars with Poland, ended by the <b>Treaty of Melno</b> in 1422, which fixed the Lithuanian border, drained its finances and alienated its subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
